
=== Part 2: Deployment with mim

This is the second of two tutorials covering the mim workflow.
link:tutorial-development.asc[Part 1] covered installation, environments,
and developing morloc programs. This part covers deploying them -- from
local serving to portable container images.

==== Where we left off

Part 1 ended with a working `dnd` environment containing a cross-language
dice roller program. The environment has root modules installed (`root`,
`root-py`, `root-r`, `root-cpp`), a custom Dockerfile layer, and the
program builds and runs successfully:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
"You rolled a 19"
----

So far the compiled binary lives in your working directory. The steps
below move it into the environment's permanent state and expose it as
an HTTP service.

==== Step 1: Install the program

To include a compiled program in the environment's permanent state -- so
the nexus can serve it, and so `freeze` can export it -- install it with
`--install`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc
----

`--install` builds the binary and compiled pools directly into the
environment's data directory, along with the files listed in
`package.yaml`'s `include` field. No build artifacts are left in the
working directory.

To reinstall after editing source code, add `--force`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc make --install --force dnd.loc
----

You can install multiple programs into the same environment. Each becomes
independently addressable when served.

==== Step 2: Serve locally

With at least one installed program, you can serve the environment over
HTTP:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim start
Starting serve container morloc-serve-weena-dnd on ports 8080:8080...
Container morloc-serve-weena-dnd started
  Logs:   mim logs
  Stop:   mim stop
  Status: mim status
----

`start` bind-mounts the environment's data directory into a container
that runs `morloc-nexus --router` as its entrypoint. No build step is
needed -- the nexus reads the installed programs at startup.

Serve a specific environment on a custom port:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim start --env dnd -p 9090:8080
----

Call the service:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ curl -s localhost:8080/health
{"programs":[{"program":"dnd","status":"ok"}],"status":"ok"}

$ curl -s localhost:8080/programs
{"programs":[{"name":"dnd","running":false,"commands":[{"name":"rollAdv","type":"remote","return_type":"Str"}]}]}

$ curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/call/dnd/rollAdv
{"status":"ok","result":"You rolled a 19"}
----

NOTE: On first start, the language daemons are initialized eagerly before
the router begins accepting requests. The `/health` endpoint will return
`"status":"ok"` once all daemons are ready. If using `/health` as a
Kubernetes readiness probe, a short `initialDelaySeconds` (2-5 seconds)
is sufficient to cover daemon startup time.

==== Step 3: Status, logs, and stop

Check which environments are being served:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim status
Running servers:
  morloc-serve-weena-dnd  0.0.0.0:8080->8080/tcp  (dnd)  [Up 5 seconds]
----

View container logs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim logs
----

Stop serving:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim stop
Stopped serving environment: dnd
----

You can also stop a specific environment by name:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim stop --env dnd
----

`start` and `stop` are symmetric -- `start` creates a serve container,
`stop` removes it.

==== Step 4: Check environment health

Before deploying externally, check that the environment is properly set
up with the `doctor` command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim doctor
Environment: dnd (local)
Engine:      podman

Prerequisites
  [ok] podman engine reachable (5.3.1)
  [ok] Base image ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.85.0
  [ok] Data directories intact

Manifests
  [ok] dnd -- built with 0.85.0, 3 pools

Deep checks
  [--] Use --deep to run container-side checks

5 passed, 0 warnings, 0 errors
----

`doctor` validates the environment in three areas:

* _Prerequisites_: Is the container engine reachable? Do the base and
  built images exist locally? Are the data directories intact?
* _Manifests_: Is each program manifest valid JSON? Does the
  `build.morloc_version` in each manifest match the environment's
  configured Morloc version? Do pool files exist?
* _Deep checks_ (`--deep`): Runs inside the container. Confirms
  `morloc --version` matches expectations, and smoke-tests each
  installed program.

The `--deep` flag starts a container for each check, so it is slower but
catches issues that host-side checks cannot -- such as a missing shared
library or a broken Python import path:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim doctor --deep
...
Deep checks
  [ok] morloc 0.85.0 (matches config)
  [ok] dnd -- smoke test passed

7 passed, 0 warnings, 0 errors
----

If `doctor` reports a version mismatch in a manifest, recompile the
affected program:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc
----

==== Step 5: Freeze for export

For deploying to a different machine, a Kubernetes cluster, or a container
registry, `mim` provides a freeze/unfreeze pipeline.

_Freeze_ packages the environment's installed state as a portable tarball
plus an auditable manifest. Before creating any artifacts, `freeze`
validates that every installed program starts correctly:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim freeze -o ./dnd-freeze
Detecting morloc version from image...
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (1 commands)
Freezing installed state from ~/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd...
Created ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz
Wrote ./dnd-freeze/freeze-manifest.json
Frozen state written to ./dnd-freeze
----

If any program fails validation (for example, a missing source file or a
broken import), `freeze` aborts before creating the tarball.

The output contains:

`state.tar.gz`:: The `lib/`, `fdb/`, `bin/`, `exe/`, `opt/`, and `src/`
directories -- everything needed to reproduce the environment at runtime.
`freeze-manifest.json`:: A record of the Morloc version, timestamp,
installed modules with SHA-256 checksums, compiled programs with their
exported commands, base image, and the Dockerfile layer used.

==== Step 6: Build a portable image

_Unfreeze_ builds a standalone container image from the frozen state and
validates the result:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim unfreeze --from ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz --tag dnd-serve:v1
Using base image: ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.85.0
Extracting frozen state...
Built serve image: dnd-serve:v1
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (1 commands)
----

If post-build validation fails, the image is kept (for debugging) but
`unfreeze` exits with a non-zero status so scripts and CI pipelines
detect the failure.

The resulting image contains the morloc-nexus router, Morloc compiler,
language runtimes, and the frozen state. It runs anywhere a container
engine runs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ podman run -d -p 8080:8080 dnd-serve:v1
$ curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/call/dnd/rollAdv
{"status":"ok","result":"You rolled a 14"}
----

Push it to a registry, deploy it to Kubernetes, or ship it to a
collaborator. The image is self-contained and does not need
`mim`.

`freeze` and `unfreeze` are symmetric: freeze exports state from an
environment into a tarball, unfreeze builds state from a tarball into an
image. Together they produce images intended for _external_ deployment.
For local serving, use `start` directly -- no freeze step needed.

==== Step 7: System-wide environments

System environments are shared across users on a machine. They require
root to create and configure, but allow non-root users to compile and run
programs. The full setup sequence is:

[%nowrap, console]
----
# 1. Create the system environment (as root). The first environment root
#    creates in the system scope also becomes the system default.
$ sudo mim new shared --system --morloc-version 0.85.0

# 2. (If root already had a system default) make `shared` the system default
$ sudo mim modify --env shared --set-default --system

# 3. Install required modules (as root) -- users cannot do this step
$ sudo mim run --env shared -- morloc install root root-py root-r root-cpp

# 4. Add users to the container engine group (for docker)
$ sudo usermod -aG docker <username>
----

Regular users then run against the shared environment with no extra step:
`mim run -- ...` targets the system default when a user has no local
environment. Step 3 is mandatory -- without it, regular users will see
`Permission denied` errors when compiling, because `morloc install` creates
directories under `/opt/morloc/` that only root can write.

NOTE: The system-wide default (set with `--set-default --system`) serves users
who have _no local default environment_. A user's own local default (set with
`mim modify --env <name> --set-default`) takes priority over the
system default. Users who only ever run against `shared` need not set anything.

System-wide environments are stored under `/etc/morloc/environments/`
(config) and `/usr/local/share/morloc/environments/` (data). They appear
in `mim ls` alongside local environments.

*Write permissions.* System environment data directories are read-only for
regular users. Operations that write to the module store -- `morloc
install` and `morloc make --install` -- will fail with permission denied.
When running `morloc make --install` as root, always `cd` into the project
directory first. By default, all files in the working directory are
bundled into the install. Running from `/root` or another home directory
will include dotfiles and other unrelated files. Use `package.yaml`'s
`include` field or a `.morlocignore` file to control which files are
bundled.
Plain `morloc make` still works: it writes the nexus binary and pool files
to the current working directory, not to the system data directory. Users
who need to install new modules should create a local environment
(`mim new myproject`) alongside the system one.

*Podman note.* Podman stores images per-user, so root's images are not
visible to regular users by default. For system environments, use Docker
instead:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ sudo mim new shared --engine docker --system
$ sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
----

Docker's socket-based model shares images across users without storage
conflicts.

NOTE: When running `sudo -u <user> mim ...` from a directory
that the target user cannot access (e.g., `/root`), the container engine
may fail with a misleading error. Run from `/tmp` or the target user's
home directory instead.

For podman setups, users do not need group membership -- podman runs
rootless by default. For docker setups, users must be in the `docker`
group.

==== Scripting notes

`mim` follows a consistent stream convention:

* _Informational commands_ (`ls`, `info`, `status`, `doctor`) write their
  output to _stdout_. This is the logical return value of the command --
  you can capture it, pipe it, or redirect it.

* _Mutational commands_ (`start`, `stop`, `freeze`, `nuke`, `update`) have
  no return value. All their output -- progress messages, success
  confirmations, warnings -- goes to _stderr_.

* _Errors and warnings_ always go to _stderr_, regardless of command type.

This means you can reliably capture query output:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ active=$(mim info | grep '^Active:' | awk '{print $2}')
$ mim ls > envs.txt
----

And suppress mutation noise without losing data:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim update --env dnd 2>/dev/null   # silent update
$ mim start 2>/dev/null        # silent start
----

==== Recap

This tutorial covered the deployment half of mim:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ mim run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc  # install permanently
$ mim start                                 # serve locally
$ mim status                                # check what's running
$ mim logs                                  # view server logs
$ mim stop                                  # stop serving
$ mim doctor --deep                         # validate health
$ mim freeze -o ./dnd-freeze                # export state
$ mim unfreeze --from ... --tag dnd-serve:v1  # build image
----

Together with Part 1, you've seen the full arc:

* _Development_ (Part 1): Create environments, install modules, write
  cross-language programs, add system dependencies, manage multiple
  environments.
* _Deployment_ (Part 2): Install programs permanently, serve over HTTP,
  check health, export portable images, set up system-wide environments
  for shared servers.

The same program can be run from the command line, served as an HTTP
API, or frozen into a portable container image -- all managed through
a single tool.
